---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { apps } from '../../data/site';
const app = apps.find((item) => item.slug === 'killmyapps')!;
---
<BaseLayout title="KillMyApps" description={app.description}>
  <div class="container page">
    <p><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← Projects</a></p>
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header"><span class="eyebrow">Android Utility</span><h1>{app.name}</h1><p>{app.tagline}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Download</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>View on GitHub</a></div></header>
    <section class="detail-intro"><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.version}</strong><span>Version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.minAndroid}</strong><span>Minimum version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>Root / Shizuku</strong><span>Execution layers</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.license}</strong><span>Open source</span></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Overview</span><h2>A small system utility.</h2><p>{app.description}</p><p>KillMyApps is built around a simple action: select an Android app and terminate it. The interface stays focused on that task instead of becoming a general app manager.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">The problem</span><h2>Direct access to app processes.</h2><p>Android devices can have applications that keep processes alive or restart in the background. For some users, reaching the relevant system action through standard settings takes more steps than necessary.</p><p>KillMyApps provides another path for terminating selected applications, with Root or Shizuku used for the privileged part of the operation.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Approach</span><h2>Focused on the core operation.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Root & Shizuku</h3><p>Privileged execution through Root or Shizuku is used for the underlying system operation.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Source-aware selection</h3><p>User, Launchable, and System sources provide different ways to find applications.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Selection Mode</h3><p>Select the applications to terminate instead of relying on a fixed list.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Automation</h3><p>Auto Killer can handle recurring background processes for users who need it.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Quick access</h3><p>Quick Settings Tile and home-screen widgets provide shortcuts to the main action.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Focused UI</h3><p>The interface stays centered on app termination rather than broader app management.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span><h2>A shared execution path.</h2><p>The application separates app selection and UI state from the privileged execution layer. Root and Shizuku integration therefore stays behind a small boundary while the rest of the UI can change independently.</p><p>Selection, filtering, automation, widgets, and Quick Settings can use the same core behavior instead of maintaining separate kill flows.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">System integration</span><h2>Using Android's process controls.</h2><p>The project works close to Android's process-management layer and uses privileged execution for operations outside the normal application sandbox.</p><p>Root and Shizuku provide two execution models without requiring separate versions of the main workflow.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Performance</span><h2>Keeping the common path responsive.</h2><p>Filtering, selection, background work, and privileged execution are kept relatively small so the main workflow does not have unnecessary work around it.</p><p>The UI follows the same approach: common information is visible, while less frequently used controls stay secondary.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Release history</span><h2>Changes over time.</h2><div class="release-list"><div><strong>v1.5</strong><span>Auto Killer, 1×1 and 1×3 widgets, Shizuku UserService/AIDL work, Android 17 support, UI and performance changes.</span></div><div><strong>v1.4</strong><span>Quick Settings Tile, Selection Mode, animation controls, Shizuku integration, filtering and performance changes.</span></div><div><strong>v1.3</strong><span>Shizuku support and Killer Mode.</span></div><div><strong>v1.2</strong><span>Further changes to the core utility and workflow.</span></div><div><strong>v1.1</strong><span>Post-stable fixes and changes to the core workflow.</span></div><div><strong>v1.0.0</strong><span>First stable release.</span></div></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Design decisions</span><h2>Keep the scope narrow.</h2><p>Features that add persistent state across widgets, Quick Settings, shortcuts, and selection UI are considered against the main use case. The project is intentionally not a full app manager.</p><p>The aim is to keep the common action straightforward while still supporting system-level Android setups.</p></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
